{"step_index":145,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-05T13:20:02Z","input_tokens":5917,"cache_read_tokens":231737,"output_tokens":2448,"thinking":"The initial data shows inconsistencies in how routes are handled across different channels. Specifically, the route assignments for Auxiliary 1 and Main LR differ from those of IP1. This disparity could indicate a misconfiguration or a fundamental difference in their intended operation, necessitating further investigation. I'll focus on the channel route setup to understand the discrepancies.\n\n\nThe data format represents gain values using `0x8000` for unity (0.0 dB) and `0x0000` for off (-Inf dB) in 16-bit unsigned integers. This convention is consistent across all gain-related parameters. The initial discrepancy in Route 32 between different channels can now be interpreted through this dB scale.\n\n\nInitial observation: Auxiliary 1 and Main LR have their master faders at unity (0.0 dB or `00 80`), which is the expected default after a reset on the target console. Input channels, conversely, default to -Inf dB when sending to Main LR (Route 32), yet the sends to groups are assigned to 0.0dB. This default behavior might provide a clue to the root of the routing discrepancies.\n\n\nThe observed default settings reveal a clear pattern. Mix master faders are consistently at unity, groups have fixed unity gain when assigned, and input channels default to -Inf dB when sending to Aux, Main LR, or FX sends with pre/post-fader assignments. These defaults, particularly the -Inf dB setting for input channel sends to outputs, appear to define the initial routing behavior. I should now verify these consistent patterns within the specific data set.\n\n\nExamining the provided data, Group routes consistently show unity gain with center panning and an unassigned post-fader setting. Auxiliary sends are set to -Inf dB, center pan, assigned, and pre-fader. The Main LR (Route 32) behaves similarly to Aux sends, at -Inf dB and post-fader.\n\nCrucially, on Auxiliary channels and Main LR, Route 32 reflects unity gain, aligning with master fader behavior. This confirms the initial hypothesis about the role of the master fader and reveals the defaults: Groups at 0 dB, Aux/LR at -Inf dB, and the channel fader at 0 dB. These differing settings point to intended routing differences.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"$bytes = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes(\"C:\\Program Files\\Allen & Heath\\SQ MixPad 1.6.1\\SQ MixPad.exe\")\n$text = [System.Text.Encoding]::ASCII.GetString($bytes, 0x268600, 0x100000)\n$idx = 0\nwhile (($idx = $text.IndexOf(\"UnitsHelper\", $idx)) -ge 0) {\n    $raw = 0x268600 + $idx\n    $sub = $text.Substring([Math]::Max(0, $idx - 100), 300) -replace \"[^\\x20-\\x7E]+\", \" | \"\n    Write-Host (\"0x{0:X6}: {1}\" -f $raw, $sub)\n    $idx += 11\n}","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Find UnitsHelper strings in SQ MixPad.exe"}}]}
